<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <assemblyIdentity version="0.1.0.0" name="SplitLane.Engine" type="win32" />

  <!--
    asInvoker, not requireAdministrator, even though diverting packets needs administrative rights.

    The engine has two modes that legitimately run unelevated, and a manifest demanding elevation
    made both of them impossible. The preflight mode exists to tell a user why the divert layer will
    not start; requiring elevation to ask that question means the one command written for a machine
    that cannot run the engine cannot itself be run. The no-divert mode runs the control channel and
    relay with no driver at all, and is how the app is developed and demonstrated on a machine
    without WinDivert installed.

    In the normal case the process checks its own token at startup and refuses with an actionable
    message rather than raising a UAC prompt, so "it silently did nothing" is not a state SplitLane
    can be in. See Program.Main.

    (Note for future edits: an XML comment may not contain two consecutive hyphens, so command-line
    switches are spelled out in words here rather than quoted literally. Writing them literally
    produces a manifest that fails to parse and an executable that will not start at all, with only
    a side-by-side configuration error to explain it.)
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 / 11 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <longPathAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">true</longPathAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
